  "image_name": "3bc394a905002eeb99d996ea9488a9e0.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how different immune cells may recognize and control an HIV‑1–infected CD4+ T cell. At the center is the infected CD4+ T cell, shown producing viral components and integrating viral DNA into the host genome, emphasizing both intracellular replication and antigen display. Around it, several immune partners interact through MHC, peptide, and receptor-mediated contacts.\n\nCD8+ cytotoxic T cells and possibly CD4+ T cells are depicted as killing infected cells by cytolysis, with dendritic cells helping prime or support these T-cell responses. B cells appear to contribute through antibody production, potentially neutralizing virus and presenting antigen to helper T cells. NK cells are shown engaging infected cells through antibody-dependent and receptor-based mechanisms, suggesting roles such as antibody-dependent cellular cytotoxicity or inhibition of viral spread. Macrophages may also participate, possibly through phagocytic clearance and their own antiviral programs.\n\nA recurring theme is “cell-intrinsic antiviral immunity” in multiple cell types, indicating that protection is not only from direct killing but also from internal antiviral defenses. Additional lymphocyte subsets, including TH17 and γδ T cells, are linked more tentatively, implying less certain or indirect contributions. Overall, the image presents HIV control as a coordinated, multi-cellular immune network.",
